 ## Overview
-Gradient-driven sampling is a ray marching technique that dynamically adjusts step sizes based on the magnitude of the scalar field gradient, refining traversal in regions with high feature sharpness while coarsening it in smooth interiors [S1]. It distinguishes itself from uniform sampling by adapting to local geometry to preserve boundary details without aliasing, and from hierarchical sampling by using gradient magnitude rather than variance or error tolerance to drive step selection [S1].
+Gradient-driven sampling is a ray marching technique that dynamically adjusts step sizes based on the magnitude of the scalar field gradient, refining traversal in regions with high feature sharpness while coarsening it in smooth interiors [S1]. It builds on [Dynamic Sampling Step Adjustment](2442-dynamic-sampling-step-adjustment.md) by adapting step sizes to local gradient magnitude rather than fixed rules [S1]. It distinguishes itself from uniform sampling by adapting to local geometry to preserve boundary details without aliasing, and from hierarchical sampling by using gradient magnitude rather than variance or error tolerance to drive step selection [S1].
 
